WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
We have the opportunity to develop the same kind of user interface

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:12.000
to test this theory with three-dimensional models that can display all kinds of objects

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.000
whether they are molecules, threads, but also readers

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:26.000
with a simple implementation which might seem unreal for  all those who are used to building user interfaces.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:34.000
My name is Guillaume Santini. I am a lecturer in computer sciences at the Université Paris 13.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.000
I am a biophysicist

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:40.000
I did a PhD in molecular modeling

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:51.000
and I participated in the development of new theoretical models of the folding of the nucleic acids DNA and RNA molecules.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:57.000
My name is Jean Cognet. I am a professor at the University Pierre et Marie Curie at Paris 6.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:05.000
I teach biostatistics, biophysics of macromolecules DNA and NRA

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:08.000
and modeling, particularly with Mathematica.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:11.000
Our team is working on the modeling of biological macromolecules.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:16.000
This is to determine the atomic conformation of large molecules

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:21.000
that's why I bring you a little bit, so it's a small piece of a small DNA fragment.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:24.000
So we had to write modeling software

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.000
This is something very complicated and heavy.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:35.000
Classical modeling software allows us to represent these types of molecules,

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:37.000
[but] the concept of elastic thread is absent,

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:44.000
and they do not allow us to represent that thread. However, we need this tool in our research

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:49.000
So there are several aspects that seem interesting to me in Mathematica.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:57.000
First from an IT perspective, we have different ways of programming in Mathematica

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:02.000
That is to say, it is quite well suitable to program with a mandatory approach

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:05.000
in languages such as C or PASCAL

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:13.000
it is also suitable for other programming approaches, such as the functional programming or approaches that are based on rewriting

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:20.000
The second advantage I see in it is the structure of the notebook

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:28.000
that allows us to detail our ideas, to write them, and to eventually test them.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:35.000
That is to say to program, obtain the results, and analyze them in the same environment.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:40.000
So there is this aspect of an integrated environment, where we can think

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:44.000
write, program, show results,

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:48.000
I would say that is conducive to effective development

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:52.000
And here I would say that thanks to Workbench

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:55.000
we finally have an effective tool to help develop the package,

00:02:55.000 --> 00:03:00.000
and even more effective than the tools developed in the Workbench

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:09.000
we can now integrate all the documentation that we developed for our own account and for third-party users.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:13.000
So from that point of view then we have tools that ultimately allow us

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:21.000
to integrate packages we develop directly into the architecture of Mathematica

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:30.000
and take advantage of all the flexibility and queries implemented in Mathematica.

